WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.240
In the texts and the accompanying book,

00:04.240 --> 00:08.520
you will observe frequent occurrences of a sequence of two identical consonant letters.

00:08.520 --> 00:14.360
It is important to remember that this indicates that the consonant sound in question is noticeably longer than for a single letter.

00:14.360 --> 00:19.480
Listen to and practice this distinction in the pairs of words that are now to be spoken.

00:19.480 --> 00:25.560
The first in each pair has a short consonant in the middle, the second a long consonant.

00:25.560 --> 00:28.160
Here is the first pair.

00:28.160 --> 00:30.200
Money.

00:30.200 --> 00:32.400
paṇam (பணம்)

00:32.400 --> 00:34.560
Make.

00:34.560 --> 00:37.000
paṇṇu (பண்ணு)

00:37.000 --> 00:40.000
Now the second pair. Mind.

00:40.000 --> 00:43.080
manam (மனம்)

00:43.080 --> 00:45.360
Cheek.

00:45.360 --> 00:48.000
kannam (கன்னம்)

00:48.000 --> 00:50.160
And another pair. Yes.

00:50.160 --> 00:53.200
aamaa (ஆமா)

00:53.200 --> 00:54.640
Mother.

00:54.640 --> 00:57.600
ammaa (அம்மா)

00:57.600 --> 01:00.000
And another. Art.

01:00.000 --> 01:02.800
kale (கலை)

01:02.800 --> 01:04.640
Stone.

01:04.640 --> 01:08.000
kallu (கல்லு)

01:08.000 --> 01:10.960
And another. Tamarind.

01:10.960 --> 01:14.800
puḷi (புளி)

01:14.800 --> 01:16.800
Dot.

01:16.800 --> 01:20.000
puḷḷi (புள்ளி)

01:20.000 --> 01:22.560
And another, to come.

01:22.560 --> 01:25.680
vara (வர)

01:25.680 --> 01:27.840
She is coming.

01:27.840 --> 01:31.280
varraa (வர்றா)

01:31.280 --> 01:34.960
And now the last pair. Usefulness.

01:34.960 --> 01:37.840
payan (பயன்)

01:37.840 --> 01:39.600
Boy.

01:39.600 --> 01:43.000
payyan (பையன்)